.Dd July 27, 2026
.Dt SECUREARC-SEAL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm securearc-seal
.Nd command-line ARC sealing tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl d Ar domain
.Fl s Ar selector
.Fl k Ar keyfile
.Op Fl a Ar authserv-id
.Op Fl -headers Ar list
.Op Fl -methods Ar list
.Op Fl t Ar timestamp
.Op Fl n Ar nameserver
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl b Ar bits
.Op Fl r Ar count
.Op Fl m Ar ms
.Ar message-file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
seals an RFC 5322 message file using the same ARC-set construction
.Xr securearc 8
uses, and prints the three new header fields to standard output, separated
by blank lines:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ARC-Authentication-Results: ...

ARC-Seal: ...

ARC-Message-Signature: ...
.Ed
.Pp
The chain already on the message is validated first, using the same
.Fn validateChain
the daemon calls, because the
.Cm cv=
this host seals is a statement about that existing chain.
An existing chain that fails to parse is treated as
.Cm cv=fail
(RFC 8617 \[sc]5.1.2), and a chain already marked
.Cm cv=fail
is not extended at all (RFC 8617 \[sc]5.1.3): in that case
.Nm
exits 0 and prints nothing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d Ar domain
Signing domain
.Pq Cm d=
for the new ARC set.
Required.
.It Fl s Ar selector
Selector
.Pq Cm s=
for the new ARC set.
Required.
.It Fl k Ar keyfile
RSA private key file, PEM format.
Required.
Held to the RFC 8301 1024-bit floor and refused if its permissions are
readable beyond its owner, exactly as
.Cm SealKeyFile
is in
.Xr securearc 8 .
.It Fl a Ar authserv-id
authserv-id for the ARC-Authentication-Results header, and the id whose
Authentication-Results this host vouches for.
Default:
.Dq securearc.test .
.It Fl -headers Ar list
Colon-separated
.Cm h=
list for the ARC-Message-Signature.
Default:
.Dq from:to:subject:date:message-id .
.It Fl -methods Ar list
Comma-separated local authentication methods this host performs.
Only Authentication-Results whose methods are all listed are sealed into the
ARC-Authentication-Results header; anything else is sender-supplied and
yields an honest
.Dq none
(RFC 8617 \[sc]5.1.1).
Default: empty, meaning nothing is sealed.
.It Fl t Ar timestamp
Signature timestamp for
.Cm t= .
Default: the current time.
Fix this to make output byte-reproducible across runs.
.It Fl n Ar nameserver
DNS nameserver used to validate the existing chain.
Default:
.Dq 127.0.0.1 .
.It Fl p Ar port
DNS nameserver port.
Default: 53.
.It Fl b Ar bits
Minimum RSA key bits accepted while validating the existing chain.
Default: 1024.
Reconciled with the RFC 8301 floor; applies only to keys published by other
ADMDs in the chain being validated, not to the signing key given with
.Fl k ,
which is always held to the floor regardless.
.It Fl r Ar count
Key records to try at one selector while validating the existing chain.
Default: 3.
Values above 8 are clamped to 8.
.It Fl m Ar ms
Wall-clock budget for validating the existing chain, in milliseconds.
Default: 20000, matching the daemon's
.Cm MaxEvaluationMs .
0 disables the limit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Display usage information.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
A new ARC set was produced and printed, or the existing chain was already
.Cm cv=fail
and nothing was sealed (RFC 8617 \[sc]5.1.3).
.It 1
The existing chain validated, but the new ARC set could not be built.
.It 2
The tool could not run: bad arguments, an unreadable or unparsable message
file, a key that could not be loaded, a transient DNS failure or evaluation
deadline while validating the existing chain, or the chain is already at the
maximum of 50 instances.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Seal a message using local resolution:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securearc-seal -d example.com -s arc2026 -k /usr/local/etc/securearc/arc.key \e
    --methods spf,dkim,dmarc message.eml
.Ed
.Pp
Produce byte-reproducible output for a conformance suite:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
securearc-seal -d example.com -s arc2026 -k arc.key -t 1700000000 \e
    -n 127.0.0.1 -p 5353 message.eml
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr securearc 8 ,
.Xr securearc-check 8 ,
.Xr securearc-testkey 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%T Authenticated Received Chain (ARC) Protocol
.%R RFC 8617
.Re
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Morante
